<h2>The course of the litigation</h2>
<p>The eviction petition — E.P. No. 1257/2016 — concerned a shop in Jyoti Colony, Shahdara. The landlord pleaded bona fide requirement under Section 14(1)(e) of the Delhi Rent Control Act, 1958: he and his son were unemployed and intended to run a confectionery business from the shop. The tenant sought leave to defend on a structurally different footing — he claimed he was no longer a tenant at all, because an agreement to sell of the year 2000 had placed him in possession as a buyer in part performance.</p>
<p>The complication was the tenant’s own litigation history. His 2012 suit for specific performance of that agreement had been dismissed as barred by limitation, and the dismissal had survived challenge up to the Supreme Court. The Rent Controller reasoned that with the suit dead, the sale-based claim raised no triable issue, refused leave to defend, and ordered eviction on 22 March 2023.</p>

<h2>The distinction the Rent Controller missed</h2>
<p>Section 53-A of the Transfer of Property Act embodies the doctrine of part performance: where a transferee has, in part performance of a written contract, taken or continued in possession and is willing to perform his side, the transferor is debarred from enforcing against him any right in respect of the property other than one expressly provided by the contract. Critically, the provision operates as a shield for the person in possession — it is not an action to enforce the contract.</p>
<div class="compare">
<div class="col old"><strong>Specific performance suit</strong><p>A sword: an action to compel conveyance, governed by its own limitation period — here, dismissed as time-barred.</p></div>
<div class="arrow">→</div>
<div class="col new"><strong>Section 53-A defence</strong><p>A shield: protection of possession taken in part performance, raised defensively against the transferor’s claim — requiring independent examination of its ingredients.</p></div>
</div>
<p>Justice Harish Vaidyanathan Shankar held that the Rent Controller collapsed the two. The dismissal of the suit on limitation did not, by itself, defeat the distinct statutory defence; the leave-to-defend application had to be tested on whether the Section 53-A plea raised a triable issue. Not examining that defence on its own footing was a flaw in the decision-making process — the kind of jurisdictional error that alone warrants interference in a rent control revision.</p>

<h2>The disposition</h2>
<p>The order of 22 March 2023 was set aside and the matter remanded for de novo consideration of the leave-to-defend application, focused on the part performance plea. The Court was careful to express no opinion on the merits — whether the agreement is genuine, whether possession was referable to it, and whether the tenant shows continued willingness to perform are all open questions for the Rent Controller, before whom the matter was listed on 29 October 2026.</p>

<h2>Practice notes</h2>
<div class="check">
<p>For tenants claiming under an agreement to sell: plead Section 53-A expressly and distinctly in the leave-to-defend affidavit — possession referable to the contract, the writing, and willingness to perform — rather than leaving it implicit in a failed specific performance narrative. For landlords: meet the defence on its ingredients; a limitation victory in the specific performance suit helps but does not conclude the Section 53-A question. For both: remember that after the 2001 amendment, an unregistered agreement generally cannot support Section 53-A, so the documentary foundation of the plea deserves early and careful attention in any such dispute.</p>
</div>
